.TH "zelta" "8" "" "" "System Manager\(cqs Manual"
.SH NAME
\f[B]zelta\f[R] \- perform safe, recursive ZFS operations locally and
remotely
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
    zelta \-V | \(rs\-\-version | version
    zelta SUBCOMMAND [OPTIONS] [ARGUMENTS]
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]zelta\f[R] provides tools for ZFS replication, backup verification,
dataset recovery, and policy\-based automation.
All operations work recursively on dataset trees and support both local
and remote execution via \f[B]ssh(1)\f[R].
.PP
Remote dataset endpoints follow \f[B]scp(1)\f[R] conventions.
Operations can be performed without installing \f[B]zelta\f[R] on remote
systems\(emonly standard ZFS utilities and SSH access are required.
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.EX
Local:  pool/dataset\(atsnapshot
Remote: user\(atexample.com:pool/dataset\(atsnapshot
IPv6:   user\(at[2001:db8::1]:pool/dataset
.EE
.PP
See \f[B]zfs(8)\f[R] for dataset naming conventions.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
For detailed usage of each subcommand, run \f[B]zelta help \f[R] or see
the respective manual page.
.TP
\f[B]zelta \-?\f[R]
Display help message.
.TP
\f[B]zelta \-V\f[R], \f[B]\-\-version\f[R], \f[B]version\f[R]
Display version information.
.SS Comparison
.TP
\f[B]zelta match\f[R] \f[I]source\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
Compare two dataset trees and report matching snapshots or
discrepancies.
See \f[B]zelta\-match(8)\f[R].
.SS Backup
.TP
\f[B]zelta backup\f[R] \f[I]source\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
Replicate a dataset tree.
Creates snapshots if needed, detects optimal send options, and
replicates intermediate snapshots.
See \f[B]zelta\-backup(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zelta snapshot\f[R] \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
Create recursive snapshots on a local or remote endpoint.
See \f[B]zelta\-snapshot(8)\f[R].
.SS Recovery
.TP
\f[B]zelta clone\f[R] \f[I]dataset\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
Create a writable clone of a dataset tree.
See \f[B]zelta\-clone(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zelta revert\f[R] \f[I]dataset\f[R]
Rewind a dataset to a previous snapshot by renaming and cloning.
Preserves current state.
See \f[B]zelta\-revert(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zelta rotate\f[R] \f[I]source\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
Preserve divergent dataset versions through rename and clone operations.
See \f[B]zelta\-rotate(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zelta failover\f[R] \f[I]source\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
Lock an active source, perform a final backup, sync local properties,
and unlock the promoted target.
See \f[B]zelta\-failover(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zelta rebase\f[R] \f[I]upstream\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
Rebase a dataset onto an upgraded upstream while preserving local files
and incremental backup continuity.
See \f[B]zelta\-rebase(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zelta lock\f[R] \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
Apply ordered dataset\-tree readonly, canmount, unmount, and remount
operations for promotion workflows.
See \f[B]zelta\-failover(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zelta unlock\f[R] \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
Reverse \f[B]zelta lock\f[R] for a promoted or maintained dataset tree.
See \f[B]zelta\-failover(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zelta propsync\f[R] \f[I]source\f[R] \f[I]target\f[R]
Replay local ZFS properties from one dataset tree to another while
preserving target\-only local overrides.
See \f[B]zelta\-failover(8)\f[R].
.SS Retention
.TP
\f[B]zelta prune\f[R] \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
Plan snapshot pruning without destroying data.
See \f[B]zelta\-prune(8)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]zprune\f[R] \f[B]\(enmatch\-endpoint=\f[R]_guard_ \f[I]endpoint\f[R]
Validate and destroy snapshots selected by \f[B]zelta prune\f[R].
See \f[B]zprune(8)\f[R].
.SS Automation
.TP
\f[B]zelta policy\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
Execute replication operations based on configuration file definitions.
See \f[B]zelta\-policy(8)\f[R].
.SH OPTIONS AND ENVIRONMENT
Configuration follows a hierarchy from lowest to highest precedence:
.IP
.EX
1. Internal defaults
2. \(ga\(ti/.config/zelta/zelta.env\(ga if present, otherwise \(ga/usr/local/etc/zelta/zelta.env\(ga
3. Policy configuration (\(gazelta.conf\(ga)
4. Environment variables
5. Command\-line arguments
.EE
.PP
See \f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R] for details.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[B]\(ti/.config/zelta/zelta.conf\f[R]
User policy configuration file, used by default when present.
.TP
\f[B]/usr/local/etc/zelta/zelta.conf\f[R]
System policy configuration file, used when user configuration is
absent.
.TP
\f[B]\(ti/.config/zelta/zelta.env\f[R]
User default setting overrides, used by default when present.
.TP
\f[B]/usr/local/etc/zelta/zelta.env\f[R]
System default setting overrides, used when user configuration is
absent.
.TP
\f[B]\(ti/.local/share/zelta/doc\f[R]
User\-installed Zelta manual root, containing \f[CR]man7\f[R] and
\f[CR]man8\f[R] directories.
.TP
\f[B]/usr/local/man\f[R]
System manual root used by root installs.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following examples use \(lqsink\(rq as the source pool and
\(lqtank\(rq as the backup target.
.PP
Replicate a dataset to a remote host:
.IP
.EX
zelta backup sink/data/project backupuser\(atbackup\-host.example:tank/backups/project
.EE
.PP
Compare local and remote dataset trees:
.IP
.EX
zelta match sink/data remote:tank/backups
.EE
.PP
Revert a dataset to its previous snapshot:
.IP
.EX
zelta revert sink/data/project
.EE
.PP
Update a target that has diverged:
.IP
.EX
zelta rotate sink/data/project backupuser\(atbackup\-host.example:tank/backups/project
.EE
.PP
Back up everything defined in policy settings (\f[B]zelta.conf\f[R]).
.IP
.EX
zelta policy
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, non\-zero on error.
.SH NOTES
See \f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R] for environment variables and
\f[CR]zelta.env\f[R] configuration.
.PP
\f[B]zelta sync\f[R] remains available for compatibility as an alias for
\f[B]zelta backup \-i\f[R].
New documentation uses explicit \f[B]zelta backup\f[R] commands.
